# =============================================================================
# .trivyignore — Temporary Trivy suppressions
# =============================================================================
#
# Rules:
#   1. Every entry MUST have an expiration date (exp:YYYY-MM-DD).
#   2. Every entry MUST link to an upstream tracking issue/advisory.
#   3. When an entry expires, re-evaluate: remove it if the CVE is fixed,
#      or extend the date with a new justification if the fix still isn't
#      available. Never extend without re-checking upstream.
#   4. Keep the file short. A growing ignorefile is a smell.
#   5. Verify against the pinned CI Trivy (the `version` default in
#      .github/actions/install-trivy/action.yml) and its DB source —
#      local `brew install trivy` uses
#      the ghcr.io DB which can classify severities differently from the
#      CI's mirror.gcr.io/aquasec/trivy-db:2.
#
# Format:
#   CVE-XXXX-XXXXX exp:YYYY-MM-DD
# =============================================================================

# CVE-2026-17106 (GHSA-hfg8-hc9c-6c3h) — github.com/moby/go-archive v0.2.0: a
# crafted tar archive can write outside the extraction directory during
# untar. Statically linked into the docker-buildx CLI plugin that
# Dockerfile.dev installs (ARG BUILDX_VERSION). Surfaced by
# security:trivy:container-scan on the GCO dev image — the only scanned image
# that carries the Buildx plugin. Fixed in moby/go-archive 0.3.0.
#
# Not introduced by any change of ours: the GHSA was published
# 2026-08-18T21:17Z and reached the Trivy DB in the following hours. The
# Security workflow was green on main at eeeab67 (2026-08-18T20:50Z), 27
# minutes before publication, so this appeared on an unchanged buildx binary.
#
# Not clearable by a stable pin bump: re-checked 2026-09-13 against the now
# current stable Buildx v0.37.1, which this repository pins. Upstream published
# go-archive v0.3.0 on 2026-07-30 (v0.3.3 by 2026-08-05), but Buildx has not
# adopted it — v0.37.1 still links v0.2.0, verified with `go version -m` on the
# published linux-amd64 binary. The advisory range is `< 0.3.0`, so the v0.37.0
# -> v0.37.1 bump neither clears nor worsens the finding.
#
# Low runtime risk: the flagged path is tar extraction. buildx is a
# build-time CLI plugin that extracts contexts and cache artifacts authored by
# the contributor or CI invoking it, plus the version-pinned images it pulls —
# not attacker-supplied archives. The dev image is a contributor/CI tool that
# is never deployed to a cluster.
#
# Clears when a buildx release ships requiring moby/go-archive >= 0.3.0: bump
# BUILDX_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev (the monthly deps-scan tracks it) and drop
# this entry. Advisory: https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-17106
CVE-2026-17106 exp:2026-11-14

# CVE-2026-14257 (GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg) — brace-expansion denial of service via
# unbounded expansion length, causing an out-of-memory process crash. Present in
# the copy npm vendors inside its own node_modules
# (usr/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/brace-expansion) in the dev image, so
# it is surfaced only by security:trivy:container-scan on Dockerfile.dev. Fixed
# upstream in brace-expansion 5.0.8.
#
# Not introduced by any change of ours: the advisory was published 2026-07-24
# and reached the Trivy DB after the last green main run, so it appeared on an
# unchanged dev image.
#
# Not clearable by a pin bump: re-checked 2026-09-01 against npm 12.0.2 (the
# release this ARG now pins) — it still bundles brace-expansion 5.0.7,
# verified in the installed tree
# (node_modules/npm/node_modules/brace-expansion/package.json). The bundled
# copy lives inside npm's published tarball, where neither `npm audit fix`
# nor an `overrides` entry can reach it.
#
# Low runtime risk: the expansion path is reached through brace globs in npm's
# own CLI argument handling, driven by the contributor or CI invocation rather
# than untrusted remote input. The dev image is a contributor/CI tool and is
# never deployed to a cluster.
#
# Clears when npm ships a release that re-vendors brace-expansion >= 5.0.8:
# bump ARG NPM_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev (plus the packageManager pins the
# consistency check holds in lockstep) and drop this entry.
# Advisory: https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-14257
CVE-2026-14257 exp:2026-10-22

# CVE-2026-69152 (GHSA-rgw5-rvv9-x895) — brace-expansion denial of service via
# unbounded intermediate arrays, bypassing the CVE-2026-14257 mitigation
# suppressed above. Published 2026-08-01, fixed upstream in brace-expansion
# 5.0.9. Same vendored copy as CVE-2026-14257: npm ships brace-expansion 5.0.7
# inside its own node_modules in the dev image, surfaced by
# security:trivy:container-scan on Dockerfile.dev. The separate root graph is
# now clear: aws-cdk-lib 2.267.0 bundles brace-expansion 5.0.9, so the lockfile
# refresh removes the matching .npm-audit-ignore entry.
#
# This image finding is not clearable by a pin bump: re-checked 2026-09-01 —
# npm 12.0.2 is still the latest npm release and still bundles 5.0.7 in its
# immutable published tarball, beyond the reach of `npm audit fix` or an
# `overrides` entry.
#
# Low runtime risk: same exposure profile as CVE-2026-14257 above — the
# expansion path is driven by contributor/CI-authored brace globs, not
# untrusted remote input, and the dev image is never deployed to a cluster.
#
# Clears when npm ships a release that re-vendors brace-expansion >= 5.0.9:
# bump ARG NPM_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev (plus the packageManager pins the
# consistency check holds in lockstep) and drop this entry.
# Advisory: https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-69152
CVE-2026-69152 exp:2026-10-22

# CVE-2026-69192 (GHSA-mwp4-54f8-5fhr) — ip-address parses leading-zero IPv4
# octets as decimal while OS resolvers treat them as octal, enabling
# SSRF/trust-boundary bypass in code that validates an address string and
# then hands it to a resolver. Fixed upstream in ip-address 10.3.1. Present
# only as ip-address 10.2.0 vendored inside npm's own node_modules
# (socks-proxy-agent -> socks -> ip-address) in the dev image, surfaced by
# security:trivy:container-scan on Dockerfile.dev.
#
# Not clearable by a pin bump: checked 2026-09-01 — npm 12.0.2 (ARG
# NPM_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev) is the latest npm release on any dist-tag
# and still bundles ip-address 10.2.0 inside its published tarball, out of
# reach of `npm audit fix` and `overrides`.
#
# Low runtime risk: the flagged path only runs when npm connects through a
# SOCKS proxy (npm's `proxy` config), and the address it parses is the
# operator-configured proxy host — not untrusted remote input. The dev image
# is a contributor/CI tool that is never deployed to a cluster.
#
# Clears when npm ships a release that re-vendors ip-address >= 10.3.1: bump
# ARG NPM_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev (plus the packageManager pins the
# consistency check holds in lockstep) and drop this entry.
# Advisory: https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-69192
CVE-2026-69192 exp:2026-10-22

# CVE-2026-73566 — node-tar denial of service: a crafted GNU L / PAX x
# long-path header with thousands of slash-separated segments drives the
# filesFilter mapHas helper into unbounded path.dirname() recursion, and the
# resulting uncatchable RangeError terminates async/stream consumers of
# tar.t()/tar.x() called with a member-selection list. Fixed upstream in tar
# 7.5.21. Present only as tar 7.5.19 vendored inside npm's own node_modules
# in the dev image, surfaced by security:trivy:container-scan on
# Dockerfile.dev. Neither repository-owned npm graph (root or
# lambda/inference-streaming-proxy) depends on tar at all.
#
# Not clearable by a pin bump: checked 2026-09-01 — npm 12.0.2 (ARG
# NPM_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev) is the latest npm release on any dist-tag
# and declares tar ^7.5.19, shipping 7.5.19 inside its published tarball,
# out of reach of `npm audit fix` and `overrides`. Vendored-tar refresh is
# tracked upstream in https://github.com/npm/cli/issues/8917. The
# DNF_SECURITY_EPOCH knob only refreshes RPM packages and cannot touch
# npm's bundled node_modules.
#
# Low runtime risk: the vulnerable path requires npm itself to list/extract
# a hostile tarball with a selection filter; in the dev image npm handles
# registry tarballs during contributor/CI installs, impact is limited to
# process termination (DoS, no code execution), and the dev image is never
# deployed to a cluster.
#
# Clears when npm ships a release that re-vendors tar >= 7.5.21: bump
# ARG NPM_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev (plus the packageManager pins the
# consistency check holds in lockstep) and drop this entry.
# Advisory: https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-73566
CVE-2026-73566 exp:2026-11-14

# CVE-2026-102276 (GHSA-6j4f-fj2g-mc7p) and CVE-2026-102278
# (GHSA-qhr7-859c-m2p7): brace-expansion denial of service by stack
# exhaustion, through recursion in parseCommaParts() and on nested brace
# groups. Fixed upstream in brace-expansion 5.0.10 and 5.0.11. Same vendored
# copy as CVE-2026-14257 and CVE-2026-69152 above: npm ships brace-expansion
# 5.0.7 inside its own node_modules in the dev image, surfaced by
# security:trivy:container-scan on Dockerfile.dev.
#
# CVE-2026-19534 (GHSA-rfgv-xxqx-mfg5): the undici WebSocket client throws an
# uncaught TypeError, ending the process, when a server's 101 response names
# a subprotocol the client never requested. Fixed upstream in undici 6.28.1.
# Present only as undici 6.27.0 vendored inside npm's own node_modules for
# node-gyp, which uses undici's fetch to download Node headers and never opens
# a WebSocket.
#
# Not introduced by any change of ours: all three were published on
# 2026-09-29. The Security workflow was green on PR #429's head (3335db58) at
# 2026-09-30T01:56Z and red on its squash merge 50d86cd2, an identical tree,
# at 09:24Z. The advisories reached the Trivy DB in between.
#
# Not clearable by a pin bump: checked 2026-09-30, npm 12.1.0 (the latest
# release) vendors brace-expansion 5.0.9 and undici 6.28.0, so bumping ARG
# NPM_VERSION from 12.0.2 clears none of the three. The root npm graph's
# separate brace-expansion copy, bundled by aws-cdk-lib, is suppressed in
# .npm-audit-ignore.
#
# Low runtime risk: the brace expansion runs on contributor/CI-authored globs
# in npm's own CLI handling, not untrusted remote input, and the undici path
# needs a WebSocket connection npm never makes. The dev image is a
# contributor/CI tool that is never deployed to a cluster.
#
# Clears when npm ships a release that re-vendors brace-expansion >= 5.0.11
# and undici >= 6.28.1: bump ARG NPM_VERSION in Dockerfile.dev (plus the
# packageManager pins the consistency check holds in lockstep) and drop these
# entries.
# Advisories: https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-102276
#             https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-102278
#             https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-19534
CVE-2026-102276 exp:2026-10-22
CVE-2026-102278 exp:2026-10-22
CVE-2026-19534 exp:2026-10-22

# CVE-2025-47273 (setuptools 70.3.0, fixed 78.1.1) and GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964
# (msgpack 1.1.2, fixed 1.2.1) — reported by security:trivy:container-scan
# against the built dev image, under its python-pkg target.
#
# Provenance, established 2026-09-08 and correcting the AWS CLI attribution
# this entry previously carried: both come from pip's vendored dependency
# manifest in the base image, at
# usr/local/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pip/_vendor/vendor.txt, which pins
#   msgpack==1.1.2
#   setuptools==70.3.0
# Trivy's python-pkg analyzer parses that file, which is why neither package has
# a dist-info of its own and neither appears in `pip list` — they are vendored,
# not installed. Reproduced on the completely unmodified base image:
#   trivy image python:3.14.7-slim
# reports exactly these two ids and nothing else, with no repository content,
# no AWS CLI and no apt package involved.
#
# Not clearable by any pin we control — checked 2026-09-08: python:3.14.7-slim
# ships pip 26.2.1, which is the newest pip on PyPI, and it still vendors both
# affected versions inside its published wheel. `pip install --upgrade pip` is a
# no-op here, and no base-image bump can help because no fixed pip exists yet.
# APT_SECURITY_EPOCH cannot reach this either: it only refreshes .deb packages,
# and this is a python-pkg finding.
#
# The earlier AWS CLI attribution was wrong, and bumping AWSCLI_VERSION
# 2.36.36 -> 2.36.41 on the strength of it did not clear either id. Recorded so
# the dead end is not re-walked: the 2.36.41 bundle and its installed
# /usr/local/aws-cli layout contain one dist-info (prompt_toolkit), zero
# egg-info, zero PKG-INFO, no msgpack file and no file containing "70.3.0", and
# a pinned-Trivy scan of that bundle is clean. Our own tree is clear too —
# msgpack appears nowhere in requirements-lock.txt and setuptools only as a
# commented "considered unsafe" marker.
#
# Low runtime risk, and for setuptools the vulnerable code is not even shipped:
#   * CVE-2025-47273 is a path traversal in setuptools.package_index.PackageIndex.
#     pip vendors pkg_resources, not setuptools — there is no
#     pip/_vendor/setuptools directory in the image, so the flagged module is
#     absent and vendor.txt records the version for provenance only.
#   * msgpack 1.1.2 code is present at pip/_vendor/msgpack, reached only through
#     cachecontrol serialising pip's own HTTP cache while pip talks to a package
#     index. Impact is a crash of pip (availability only, no code execution).
#   * The dev image is contributor/CI tooling that is never deployed to a
#     cluster, and neither path runs as a network service.
#
# Clears when pip re-vendors msgpack >= 1.2.1 and setuptools >= 78.1.1 (or drops
# the setuptools line): pull a base image carrying that pip, confirm with
# `trivy image` on the base alone, then drop both ids. Because the finding is
# entirely upstream of this repository, a published VEX statement would be the
# durable alternative to re-dating this entry.
#
# Advisories: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2025-47273
#             https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964
CVE-2025-47273 exp:2026-11-14
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 exp:2026-11-14

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CVE-2026-97687 (traffic interception via an HTTPS proxy TLS configuration
# override) and CVE-2026-97689 (denial of service through unbounded memory
# allocation in the chunked-transfer parser) — urllib3 2.7.0, fixed in 2.8.0.
# Reported by security:trivy:container-scan against the dev image
# (Dockerfile.dev) and the two Lambda images built on
# public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.14 (helm-installer, tls-certificate-manager),
# under the pathless "Python (python-pkg)" target.
#
# Provenance, established 2026-10-01: the same mechanism as the entry above.
# pip 26.2.1 vendors urllib3 and records it in
# pip/_vendor/vendor.txt as `urllib3==2.7.0`; Trivy parses that manifest, so
# the finding has no dist-info of its own. Reproduced on the unmodified base
# images: `pip --version` reports 26.2.1 in both python:3.14.7-slim
# (/usr/local/lib/python3.14/site-packages) and public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.14
# (/var/lang/lib/python3.14/site-packages), and both vendor.txt files pin
# urllib3==2.7.0. The images that keep no pip in their final stage do not
# report it. Our own urllib3 is 2.8.0 everywhere Trivy can see it:
# requirements-lock.txt, every lambda/*/requirements.txt, and each image's
# urllib3-2.8.0.dist-info scans clean in the same reports.
#
# Not clearable by any pin we control — checked 2026-10-01: pip 26.2.1
# (2026-08-04) is the newest pip on PyPI, and pip's main branch still vendors
# urllib3 2.7.0, so no fixed pip exists to upgrade to. A base-image bump cannot
# help until pip re-vendors, and APT_SECURITY_EPOCH only refreshes .deb packages.
#
# Low runtime risk: pip's vendored urllib3 runs only while pip itself talks to
# a package index, which the Lambda images never do after build and which the
# dev image does only while a contributor installs packages. Neither CVE is
# reachable through the application code, which imports the fixed 2.8.0.
#
# Clears when pip re-vendors urllib3 >= 2.8.0: pull a base image carrying that
# pip, confirm with `trivy image` on the base alone, then drop both ids. Expires
# together with the sibling pip vendor.txt entry above so the two are
# re-evaluated in one pass.
#
# Advisories: https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-97687
#             https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-97689
CVE-2026-97687 exp:2026-11-14
CVE-2026-97689 exp:2026-11-14
